---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/design/intermediate/ux-research">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate Design lesson, and it builds directly on this subject's foundational
    lesson, design-is-deciding — user needs, the context of use, and the door that lies. There
    the question was what a good design decision looks like; here it is how evidence reaches
    that decision. Nothing technical is assumed — the instruments are conversations, tasks and
    counts, and the hard part is discipline, not equipment.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>write a research plan that names the decision a study serves, and choose methods to fit the question;</li>
    <li>run a behavioural interview — past behaviour over predictions, the 5-whys ladder beneath feature requests;</li>
    <li>run a five-at-a-time usability test: tasks not instructions, productive silence, severity-rated findings;</li>
    <li>read analytics, A/B tests and surveys for what each can and cannot establish;</li>
    <li>triangulate what people say, do and choose — and report findings that change a decision.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    UX research is the discipline's reality check: <strong>watching real people try to use the
    thing before shipping it</strong> — the evidence-based-practice culture of the medicine
    lesson, at product speed. Its two grand divisions run along the syllabus's design
    process: <strong>generative</strong> research (identifying and defining — what do people
    need, where does the current world hurt?) and <strong>evaluative</strong> research
    (testing and evaluating — does this design work for them?). Between them sits the
    profession's core discipline: asking questions whose answers change a decision, and
    choosing methods for the question rather than the fashion. The page walks the full
    cycle — identify, define, propose, develop, plan, test — with each method's honest
    limits attached, because the field's failure mode is not too little research but
    the wrong research confidently reported.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="generative or evaluative? file each method" widget="sort-buckets" config={uxrGenEvalSort} />

  <Widget no="1" caption="decision-first questions, behavioural interviews, five users, triangulation" widget="equationStep" config={uxrCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four cards and watch each one restate a
    discipline rather than a technique — the question must name a pending decision, interviews
    collect behaviour rather than opinions, five users on real tasks beat fifty on a
    questionnaire, and the three evidence sources are compared, never averaged. Notice too that
    each card names the lesson it borrows its rigour from: the methods change, the discipline
    repeats.
  </p>

  <h2>Identify and define: decision-first research</h2>
  <p>
    Every study starts with the pending decision: which pricing page ships? does
    onboarding need a rebuild? is this feature worth building at all? The
    <strong>research plan</strong> states it explicitly — question, decision it informs,
    method, participants, timeline — because a study that cannot name its decision
    tends to produce interesting trivia. Method follows question: <em>why</em>
    questions want interviews and field studies; <em>how well</em> questions want
    usability tests; <em>how many / which</em> questions want analytics and
    experiments. Interviews are generative research's instrument, and their craft
    rule is behavioural: <strong>"tell me about the last time you…"</strong> beats
    "would you use…", because past behaviour is data and predictions about
    hypothetical futures are noise (Rob Fitzpatrick's <em>The Mom Test</em> is the
    pocket manual: ask about specifics, the present and the past; avoid compliments
    and pitches). The <strong>5-whys ladder</strong> (Toyota's root-cause habit,
    productised) digs beneath requests — "you should add an export button" becomes,
    four whys later, "I lose two hours a week rebuilding reports my manager could
    have read in the tool" — an entirely different (and better) design brief.
    Synthesis closes the defining stage: notes clustered into themes
    (affinity mapping), needs stated as problems, sometimes as
    <strong>Jobs-to-be-Done</strong> ("when commuting before work, I hire a
    thick milkshake because it occupies a boring drive and holds until
    lunch" — Christensen's founding case), and a problem statement the
    team can argue with.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="pair each question with its instrument" widget="match" config={uxrQuestionMatch} />

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> research starts from a pending decision, and interviews dig from requests to underlying needs — generative work that defines the problem. Next comes evaluative work: putting a design in front of people and watching what actually breaks.</p>

  <h2>Propose and develop: usability testing, five at a time</h2>
  <p>
    Evaluative research's workhorse is the <strong>usability test</strong>: a
    participant, real tasks, a facilitator trained to silence. The famous
    arithmetic is Nielsen and Landauer's: each participant independently
    uncovers roughly a third of a design's fixable problems, so
    <strong>five users catch most of what matters</strong> — and the correct
    conclusion is not "five is enough" but <em>five at a time, then fix, then
    five again</em>: small, fast, iterative beats large, slow, single-shot.
    (Honest footnote: later replications show high variance — five finds
    <em>most</em> severe issues on average, not a guarantee — which is an
    argument for iteration, not against.) The craft rules that make sessions
    informative: give <strong>tasks, not instructions</strong> ("pay this
    invoice", never "click the payments tab"); <strong>stay silent</strong> —
    the moment you help, you are testing your explanation, not the design;
    ask users to <strong>think aloud</strong> (Ericsson and Simon's protocol:
    report what you attend to, don't analyse); watch for hesitation, wrong
    menus, backtracking — <em>where users struggle is the finding</em>, and a
    task completed "successfully" after three wrong turns is not success.
    Rate findings by severity (cosmetic → critical), fix the criticals, and
    retest — the loop is the method.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="sequence one round of usability testing" widget="order" config={uxrSessionOrder} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a five-user study is unscientific — real research needs dozens of participants for statistical significance. That confuses two genres: usability testing is qualitative problem discovery, where the unit of analysis is the design's failure modes rather than opinions to average; the Nielsen–Landauer arithmetic works because problems repeat across users. Sample-size rules return the moment you measure rates — surveys, analytics, A/B tests — which is why mature teams pair the two instead of ranking them.</p>

  <h2>Test and evaluate: analytics, A/B tests, and the survey's honest place</h2>
  <p>
    Behaviour leaves two other traces. <strong>Analytics</strong> (funnels,
    session recordings, heatmaps) shows WHAT at population scale — where users
    drop, where rage-clicks cluster — but never WHY; the professional pairing is
    quantitative location followed by qualitative explanation. The <strong>A/B
    test</strong> (randomise two variants, compare outcomes) is the discipline's
    experiment, with the statistics lesson's traps attached: decide the sample
    size before starting, resist <em>peeking</em> (stopping at the first
    significant-looking hour produces false positives), and remember that
    statistical significance is not business importance. <strong>Surveys</strong>
    scale attitudes — and measure them badly when drafted carelessly: leading
    questions, response bias, the unrepresentative self-selection of people
    with time and opinions. Instruments like the <strong>System Usability
    Scale</strong> (ten standard items, score out of 100, ~68 as the industry
    average) exist precisely to avoid bespoke questionnaire chaos. And the
    master discipline is <strong>triangulation</strong>: what people SAY
    (interviews, surveys), what they DO (analytics, recordings), what they
    CHOOSE (A/B outcomes) are three different measurements, they disagree
    regularly, and each disagreement is a finding pointing at an assumption
    — never something to average away.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="call the SUS industry average" widget="estimate" config={uxrSusEst} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a winning A/B test explains why the variant won. It does not — a randomised comparison measures the outcome, and the mechanism stays dark without qualitative work (session recordings, interviews) to unpack it. Shipping the winner is legitimate; generalising the "lesson" to the next design without understanding the cause is how teams cargo-cult their own results.</p>

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> interviews supply the why, usability tests locate the struggle, and analytics, A/B tests and surveys each measure a different slice of behaviour — triangulated, not averaged. Before any of that lab work comes the unglamorous half that decides whether it is valid at all.</p>

  <h2>Planning and ethics: the unglamorous half</h2>
  <p>
    Research quality is set before the first participant. <strong>Recruiting</strong>
    defines validity: participants must resemble the actual audience (a "usability
    test" of your product on five design-school colleagues tests your office, not
    your market); screeners and incentives are the machinery; a mix of sessions —
    moderated, unmoderated, remote, in-context — covers what each misses.
    <strong>Ethics</strong> is not paperwork: informed consent (what is recorded,
    how data is used, the right to stop), privacy of recordings, and care with
    vulnerable participants are the profession's IRB-conscience, and
    <strong>research operations</strong> (consent flows, secure repositories,
    participant panels) is how those values survive scale. One psychological
    honesty note belongs here: participants behave differently when observed
    (<strong>observation effects</strong> — polite cooperation, self-presentation;
    the Hawthorne lineage) — which is why in-context field studies (the
    anthropology lesson's ethnography, product edition) complement lab tests:
    the workaround taped to the monitor that nobody mentions in an interview
    only exists to be visited.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="plan a field study — pick the path" widget="branch" config={uxrFieldBranch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "we tested it on users and they liked it" is
  validation. Liking is an attitude; the questions that matter are behavioural —
  could they find it, did they complete the task, what did they do instead? A
  test that collects compliments has measured politeness. The professional
  deliverable is a decision changed by evidence, and the field's culture is
  built to make that sentence, not the session count, the unit of success.</p>

  <h2>Contextual inquiry and diary studies: research where the life is</h2>
  <p>
    The lab cannot visit the kitchen, and some truths only live there.
    <strong>Contextual inquiry</strong> (the usability-engineering tradition's
    ethnographic wing) watches people work in their own environment: the
    master-apprentice stance — watch first, ask in the moment, interpret
    together — surfaces the workarounds, sticky notes and shadow systems
    that never appear in an interview ("we just keep the real rota in a
    spreadsheet and use the official tool for the boss"). <strong>Diary
    studies</strong> trade depth for duration: participants record their
    own behaviour over days or weeks (prompts, photos, short entries),
    which captures low-frequency events a session can never schedule —
    the monthly payday routine, the annual tax scramble, the moment
    a product is abandoned. Both methods answer questions the lab
    structurally cannot: what does the context do to the behaviour,
    and what do people do when nobody is watching the study?
    The cost is analysis weight — field notes and diary entries
    need systematic synthesis (affinity mapping, timeline
    reconstruction) or they become anecdotes — which is why
    these methods pair with the defining stage's synthesis
    discipline rather than replacing the fast loop of
    five-user testing.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="find the faulty line — in-context methods" widget="spot-error" config={uxrContextSpot} />

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> the lab cannot visit the kitchen — contextual inquiry and diary studies capture what only exists in context, and their weight must be synthesised systematically. Those findings still need numbers to be compared against, which is where the metric taxonomy earns its keep.</p>

  <h2>Measuring experience: the metric taxonomy</h2>
  <p>
    Evaluative research earns its keep when numbers are chosen to match the
    question. The behavioural standards: <strong>task success rate</strong>
    (did they complete it — and at what cost?), <strong>time on task</strong>
    (efficiency, with the caveat that faster is not always better on
    exploration tasks), <strong>error count and type</strong> (where the
    design confuses). The attested-attitude standards: the
    <strong>System Usability Scale</strong> (10 fixed items, benchmarked
    against decades of data — a 68 average, 80+ strong), plus task-level
    confidence and easiness ratings. Product metrics complete the picture:
    adoption, retention, funnel conversion — with the standing warning that
    engagement numbers reward addiction as readily as value, and the
    professional obligation to ask what the metric should be measuring
    before the dashboard chooses for you. Two habits keep the taxonomy
    honest: benchmark against <em>known</em> baselines (SUS means
    something because its distribution is public; a bespoke
    satisfaction score means nothing until you have history),
    and never let a single number carry a decision — the
    triangulation rule applies within metrics as much as
    across methods.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="behavioural vs attitudinal metrics — drag the divider" widget="compare" config={uxrMetricsCompare} />

  <h2>From findings to decisions: the reporting discipline</h2>
  <p>
    Research that does not change a decision is expense. The reporting
    craft starts with severity, not anecdotes: rank findings by
    frequency × severity × impact on the decision, lead with the
    answer to the question the study was commissioned to answer,
    and attach the evidence (clips, quotes, numbers) beneath each
    claim — a finding without its evidence is an opinion with a
    title. Readouts work best as decisions-in-progress: present
    the options the evidence supports, name what would change
    the conclusion, and separate observations from
    recommendations (the team owns the design; research
    owns the evidence). Writing it down is half the value:
    repositories of past findings compound — the question
    you answer rigorously once answers it for every
    future team. And the closure habit that defines
    the mature practice: revisit the decision after
    launch (did the metric move? did the fix hold?),
    so the research function calibrates itself
    against reality — the same loop the medicine
    lesson's evidence-based practice runs, at
    product speed.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="fill the blanks — the reporting discipline" widget="cloze" config={uxrReportCloze} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A user says: "You should add an export button." What do you ask next, and why?</li>
    <li>Why can five users uncover most severe usability problems while a survey needs hundreds of respondents?</li>
    <li>Interviews say a feature is loved; analytics show almost nobody uses it. What is the finding?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Ask about the last time they needed to get data out — past behaviour is data, predictions are noise, and the job beneath the request may suggest a better design than the one requested. (2) Problem discovery is about repetition of shared failures, not opinion sampling: each user independently reveals about a third of the fixable problems, so discovery saturates fast — the right move is five at a time, then fix, then five again. (3) The divergence itself: attitudes and behaviour have split (social politeness, memory bias, or a feature loved but not needed), and the next study investigates why the gap exists rather than trusting either source alone.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Research serves a pending decision; method follows the question, never the fashion.</li>
    <li>Ask about the past — "tell me about the last time…" — because predictions about hypothetical futures are noise.</li>
    <li>Five users at a time, then fix, then five again: iteration beats sample size.</li>
    <li>Give tasks, not instructions, and stay silent — the hesitation and backtracking are the data.</li>
    <li>What people say, do and choose are different measurements; each disagreement is a finding.</li>
    <li>Liking is an attitude; task success, error count and time on task are evidence.</li>
    <li>Report by severity with evidence attached, and revisit the decision after launch.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling intermediate lessons build the making side this research feeds:
    prototyping-and-wireframes turns findings into testable drafts; accessibility covers the
    failure modes five-user sessions surface most reliably; and grids-and-layout and
    typography-fundamentals supply the craft for fixing what the sessions reveal.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The five-user debate, maturely held.</strong> The Nielsen–Landauer
    model assumes problems are shareable and users independent; real studies
    show variance is huge (Faulkner's 2003 replications: five users sometimes
    miss 35% of problems). The settled practice: five finds the <em>common</em>
    problems fast; complex, heterogeneous audiences need per-audience sessions;
    critical-risk designs (medical, financial) need more eyes per round. The
    deeper point survives every replication — iteration beats sample size,
    because designs change between rounds and each round reveals what the
    previous redesign exposed.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Research at organisational scale.</strong> Mature practice builds
    <strong>research repositories</strong> (searchable archives of findings, so
    questions are answered once), <strong>opportunity solution trees</strong>
    (Teresa Torres: mapping desired outcomes to opportunities to experiments —
    continuous discovery instead of periodic studies), and the important
    distinction between <em>research</em> (rigorous, decision-changing) and
    <em>research theatre</em> (sessions performed to confirm a decision already
    made — the field's occupational hazard, detectable by whether any finding
    could have killed the plan).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Can AI-moderated interviews and synthetic
    participants extend reach (so far: useful for scale and screening,
    unreliable where empathy and context carry the signal — the ethics of
    synthetic data is the live debate)? How do privacy regimes (GDPR-era
    consent, recording laws) reshape session research as it globalises? And
    can behavioural metrics ever capture the aesthetic dimensions of design —
    or does the discipline need to keep arguing for what instruments cannot
    yet count? The fundamentals, meanwhile, are stable across tooling: a
    decision, a question, a method honest about its limits, and the
    discipline to let the evidence change the plan — four sentences that
    summarise the whole profession, and the reason this lesson is the
    design ladder's quiet capstone.
  </p>
